[{'title': 'Introduction', 'node_id': '0000', 'page_index': 1, 'key_items': ['Abstract'], 'summary': "The text is a preprint paper introducing GraphRAG, a graph-based retrieval-augmented generation approach for query-focused summarization over large private text corpora. It includes the title, authors, Microsoft affiliations, equal contribution note, arXiv identifier, and under-review status. The abstract explains that conventional RAG retrieves relevant information for local questions but fails on global sensemaking questions over an entire corpus, and that prior query-focused summarization methods do not scale. GraphRAG addresses this by using an LLM to build an entity knowledge graph and hierarchical community summaries; at query time it uses map-reduce over community summaries to produce partial answers and then a final global answer. The paper claims substantial improvements over vector RAG in answer comprehensiveness and diversity for global questions on million-token datasets. The introduction defines RAG, context windows, vector RAG, and sensemaking, and describes GraphRAG's knowledge graph construction, hierarchical community summarization, and map-reduce query answering. It also presents an LLM-as-a-judge evaluation method for ground-truth-free global questions and reports that GraphRAG strongly outperforms vector RAG with GPT-4 on two real-world datasets. The text notes GraphRAG is open source and available in LangChain, LlamaIndex, NebulaGraph, and Neo4J. The background section defines RAG systems, explains when RAG is appropriate, and contrasts conventional vector retrieval with GraphRAG's global sensemaking capability."}, {'title': 'Background', 'node_id': '0001', 'page_index': 2, 'key_items': ['RAG Approaches and Systems', '2 Background', 'Using Knowledge Graphs with LLMs and RAG; Adaptive benchmarking for RAG Evaluation', 'Using Knowledge Graphs with LLMs and RAG', 'Adaptive benchmarking for RAG Evaluation', 'RAG evaluation criteria', '2.2 Using Knowledge Graphs with LLMs and RAG', '2.3 Adaptive benchmarking for RAG Evaluation'], 'summary': 'The text presents GraphRAG, a graph-based retrieval-augmented generation approach for answering global sensemaking queries over large corpora. It contrasts GraphRAG with conventional vector RAG, describes the pipeline from text chunking and LLM-based entity/relationship extraction to hierarchical community detection, bottom-up community summaries, and map-reduce query answering. It also introduces an adaptive LLM-as-a-judge benchmarking method, reports that GraphRAG outperforms vector RAG with GPT-4, and notes open-source availability. Background sections cover RAG systems, advanced RAG and knowledge graph approaches, adaptive benchmarking, and evaluation criteria, while the methods section details source text chunking and knowledge extraction steps.'}, {'title': 'Methods', 'node_id': '0002', 'page_index': 4, 'prefix_summary': 'The Methods section describes the GraphRAG workflow from document chunking and extraction of entities, relationships, and claims through knowledge graph construction, hierarchical community detection, community summarization, and query-time multi-stage global answer generation. It presents LLM-based generation of global sensemaking questions using personas, tasks, and whole-corpus questions, with 125 questions per dataset, and defines head-to-head LLM evaluation criteria including comprehensiveness, diversity, empowerment, and directness control. It details hierarchical community partitioning and report-like summarization at leaf and higher levels, the multi-stage global query answering process, and Experiment 1 datasets (podcast transcripts and a news benchmark) comparing six conditions: GraphRAG at four community levels, text summarization, and vector RAG semantic search with context window and prompts held constant.', 'nodes': [{'title': 'GraphRAG Workflow', 'node_id': '0003', 'page_index': 4, 'key_items': ['Source Documents –> Text Chunks', 'Text